From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathieu GRENET <mathieu@mgth.fr>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 00:49:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 27/28] ao/asio: build against the ASIO SDK headers, under
 GPLv3
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ao_asio.h declared the whole ASIO ABI by hand — types, structs, enums,
sample-type and selector constants — because linking Steinberg's SDK would
have forced GPLv3 on libmpv, which upstream mpv cannot accept. That
constraint does not apply to this fork: -Dgpl=false is never used here, the
Windows build already enables dvdnav (a GPL-only file), and the libmpv asset
is already published as GPL-3.0-or-later because liborender is.

So take the SDK. Steinberg dual-licensed it on 2025-10-15 under either its
proprietary licence or GPLv3, and the GPLv3 half is one mpv can use. The 16
types and constants ao_asio.h used to declare all come from <asio.h>
verbatim, so the hand-written surface drops from 232 lines to the IASIO vtbl
alone.

That vtbl has to stay: the SDK declares IASIO in iasiodrv.h as a C++ class
with virtual methods, which a C translation unit cannot include. What remains
is the COM vtbl that class produces, and its slot order is dictated by the
ABI rather than chosen.

Only headers are used, none of the SDK sources are compiled — so this does
not drag in libstdc++, nor the two Microsoft-copyright COM base-class files
(combase.cpp, dllentry.cpp) that carry no licence grant.

Build: -Dasio now requires -Dgpl=true, following the ao_jack pattern, plus a
new -Dasio-sdk pointing at the SDK root. ao_asio.c/h are listed in Copyright
as GPL-only, with a note that they are the one GPLv3+ entry in a list that is
otherwise GPLv2+.

Cross-compiled against the real SDK headers with mingw-w64, -Wall -Wextra,
clean.
---
 Copyright           |  11 +++
 audio/out/ao_asio.c |  24 +++---
 audio/out/ao_asio.h | 194 +++++++-------------------------------------
 meson.build         |  23 +++++-
 meson.options       |   4 +-
 5 files changed, 79 insertions(+), 177 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Copyright b/Copyright
index 61d3a038e7..e4ba143f08 100644
--- a/Copyright
+++ b/Copyright
@@ -52,6 +52,8 @@ at all.
 
 The following files are still GPL only (-Dgpl=false disables them):
 
+    audio/out/ao_asio.c             GPLv3+ (see below)
+    audio/out/ao_asio.h             GPLv3+ (see below)
     audio/out/ao_jack.c             will stay GPL
     audio/out/ao_oss.c              will stay GPL
     stream/dvb*                     must stay GPL
@@ -76,3 +78,12 @@ The following files are still GPL only (-Dgpl=false disables them):
 None of the cases listed above affect the final binary if it's built as
 LGPL. Linked libraries still can affect the final license (for example if
 FFmpeg was built as GPL).
+
+ao_asio is the one GPL-only file that is GPLv3+ rather than GPLv2+: it builds
+against Steinberg's ASIO SDK headers, which Steinberg dual-licensed on
+2025-10-15 under either its proprietary ASIO license or GPLv3. mpv can only
+use the GPLv3 half, so enabling -Dasio makes the resulting binary GPLv3+
+rather than GPLv2+. This is legal because everything else in mpv is "version
+2 or later", but it does narrow what the binary can be combined with, and it
+is why -Dasio requires -Dgpl=true and a -Dasio-sdk path. None of the SDK's own
+sources are compiled: only its headers are used.
